  "summary": "New work links lineage diversification to labile traits and demography, from Mimosa niche shifts to drift in island kingfishers.",
  "synthesis": "The items address how selection, drift and ecological context structure phenotypic and lineage diversity, with no single mechanism dominating. Mimosa shows a continuum of diversification rates, with radiations accounting for most diversity and disparity and tied to abiotic niche lability. In island kingfishers, faster plumage color evolution tracks faster diversification and accelerates as genomic diversity declines, implicating drift. Bark anoles show convergent signaling traits decoupled from genomic species boundaries, with gene flow shaping speciation outcomes. Analysis of iridescent plumage across 3,151 bird species supports both Darwinian and Wallacean explanations for sexual dichromatism. In leaf-footed bugs, sexual dimorphism is labile and often arises already dimorphic in new traits. Together they emphasize lability and demographic context over uniform selective explanations.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
